Testing PAST
============

    t/run

runs the whole harness (a few minutes).  It syntax-checks snapshot.pl,
prune.pl and makelinks.pl, then runs t/harness.pl, which

  - builds a fake wwPDB site (holdings files, small gzip files, an index
    listing with relative links, a sequence file, an on-hold file) and
    serves it with t/mockserver.py on a free port of 127.0.0.1;
  - builds an empty archive root;
  - runs snapshot.pl, prune.pl and makelinks.pl against them, with standard input not
    a terminal, and checks the results.

Nothing outside the work directory is read or written, and no request
leaves the machine.  The output is TAP: one "ok" line per case, with the
individual checks indented below it.  The exit status is 0 only if every
check passed.

Needs: perl (with the modules snapshot.pl needs, and Test::More,
IO::Compress::Gzip, JSON::PP), python3 (standard library only), gzip and
timeout in the path.

Environment:

    PERL             perl to use (default /lab/bin/perl, else perl)
    PAST_TEST_TMP    where to make the work directory (default: system tmp)
    PAST_TEST_KEEP   set to keep the work directory afterwards
    PAST_TEST_SHOW   set to print the output of every snapshot.pl run
    PAST_SNAPSHOT, PAST_PRUNE, PAST_MAKELINKS   test other copies of the scripts

snapshot.pl runs with its default guards and limits; a case passes an
option only where it is about that option, or where the tiny site
would trip a fraction (one entry of twelve is 8%).

Cases (50; t/harness.pl has the details):

  start        latest missing without --init; --init with a failing
               guard (nothing created, not even data/); first run;
               same-day rerun; --init on an archive with snapshots;
               a first run that failed, run again with --init and with
               --init --fresh; --adopt when the first run published
               but there is no "latest"
  identity     nothing changed; changed content; Last-Modified changed
               only; multipart ETag; same content compressed
               differently; weak ETag, empty ETag, no Content-Length;
               32-hex ETag that is not the MD5 and --etag-not-md5
  downloads    truncated body; corrupt gzip; no Last-Modified; 404 with
               bounded retries; --max-failures; --max-changed;
               --max-hours; --max-compared; a one-part multipart ETag
               that the download does not give; a two-part one that
               cannot be checked (accepted and counted)
  guards       unparseable date; no pdb key; collapsed count; deletions;
               candidates; a kind that had no files; entry listed
               nowhere; entry that lost its PDB-format file (not written
               to "removed"); entry that becomes obsolete
  reruns       same day and next day, files and HEAD answers kept;
               SIGKILL; SIGTERM and SIGINT; lock held; more than one
               staging directory; --fresh; finished snapshot newer than
               latest and --adopt; output to a closed pipe; a space in
               the root and temporary directory names; a staged file
               with a multipart ETag that was changed after it was
               fetched (fetched again), one that was not (kept), one
               with a weak ETag (fetched again)
  coming back  a removed entry listed again, recognised from
               REMOVED.tsv and from an old-style "removed" list, linked
               to the archived copy; a REMOVED.tsv with a bad storage
               column
  damage       archived file missing (one: repaired with the recorded
               modification time; many: stop; unreadable: error);
               manifest with a wrong size and empty file with
               --refetch; truncated file with --refetch (replaced);
               file with other content of the recorded size: found by
               --manifest-only --verify, by a run with --verify and
               when it is downloaded to compare, replaced with
               --refetch; --refetch of a good file (kept)
  other        index listing (relative links, missing name, a .gz name,
               a clash); on-hold not FASTA; --dry-run and --list;
               previous snapshot without a manifest; --manifest-only
               (complete, build, resume, truncated archived file);
               --check-all; --hash-reused; --verify on a good archive
  makelinks.pl remakes a pruned tree identically from the manifest
  frozen (read-only) snapshot: a later run links into it and leaves it
    unchanged; prune.pl, makelinks.pl and --manifest-only refuse it
  prune.pl     refuses latest, trailing slash, paths, "..", symlinks;
               prunes an old snapshot

Not covered by a test:

  - the sync calls (files and directories), and anything about what
    survives a power failure;
  - a failing utime, unlink, rename or symlink (among them the checked
    removal of stale lists from a resumed staging directory, and a
    failure between publishing and switching "latest", which the --adopt
    cases imitate by removing or moving the link afterwards);
  - that the lock is taken before the archive is read: the lock case
    only shows that a run stops while another holds the lock; two runs
    racing are not tried, nor the first run in a new archive against
    another first run;
  - a real multipart ETag of more than one part: no test file is 8 MiB,
    so the part sizes other than "one part" are not exercised;
  - --refetch where the archived file is as recorded and the server's
    file differs (stored as changed), where nothing is recorded for the
    file, and for a file that comes back; a file whose MD5 is as
    recorded and whose SHA-256 is not;
  - --verify with a previous snapshot that has no manifest;
    --manifest-only --verify together with --manifest-out, and on a
    manifest with missing checksums;
  - --max-compared with its default (100), and --check-all against a
    server without usable ETags;
  - frozen snapshots as root: the harness does not run as root, so the
    refusal that does not rely on the permission check is tested only
    with a snapshot whose top directory alone is read-only;
  - the real wwPDB servers: every request in the harness goes to
    t/mockserver.py.

To try snapshot.pl by hand against the fake site, keep the work directory
(PAST_TEST_KEEP=1), start the server yourself

    python3 t/mockserver.py WORK/docroot WORK/control

and pass --root WORK/root --source http://127.0.0.1:PORT/pub/pdb
--onhold-url http://127.0.0.1:PORT/onhold --tmpdir WORK/tmp, where PORT
is in WORK/control/port.

A dry run against the real server reads the archive but writes nothing
in it:

    snapshot.pl --dry-run --list FILE
